<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s1">
  <p class="st-k rv">01 · Challenge</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s1">Finding blood meant asking around</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    When someone needs blood, they have to ask around to find a donor. BloodConnect lets a seeker
    post a request and lets donors respond to it, in a collaboration between Craftsmen and UXBD.
  </p>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Brief 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Client requirements</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">We worked to the client’s requirements throughout the project.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Brief 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Stakeholder features</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">The feature requests came from the stakeholder.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Brief 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Beta first</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">A beta was released first.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="st-callout rv">
    <p class="st-bh">
      Phase 1 had one job: get people into the app safely and correctly, from the splash screen to
      their settings.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s2">
  <p class="st-k rv">02 · Research and discovery</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s2">Three sources, three kinds of user</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    We started from the stakeholder’s feature requests and added our own desk research and
    interviews. Together they fed the user stories.
  </p>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">What we drew on</h3>
  <ol class="st-list st-wide rv">
    <li>The stakeholder’s feature requests</li>
    <li>A review of Blood Donor App (Save Life Co)</li>
    <li>A review of BloodLine (Blood Bank App BD)</li>
    <li>Interviews with real donors and seekers</li>
  </ol>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">User stories for three types of user</h3>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">User 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Donor</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">User 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Searcher</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">User 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Organization</h3>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure class="cs__fig st-wide rv">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/bloodconnect-research.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1200"
      height="900"
      alt="Diagram: stakeholder feature requests, desk research and interviews lead to user stories for donors, seekers and organizations, beside a phone showing the My Activity screen"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>Three sources fed the user stories for each type of user</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s3">
  <p class="st-k rv">03 · Defining the experience</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s3">Two journeys into the app</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    We mapped the flows with sitemaps and phased wireframes (Phase 1, Phase 2 and first release),
    then refined the UI across several versions up to handoff.
  </p>
  <div class="st-stats st-wide rv">
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">6</p>
      <p>months from research to handoff</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">2</p>
      <p>UI/UX designers, working together</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">3</p>
      <p>types of user: Donor, Searcher and Organization</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="st-grid st-g2 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Journey 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Login</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        Splash screen, then getting started with account creation, login and guest access, through
        login, forgot password, OTP and set new password.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Journey 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Registration</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Create account, OTP, set password, personal info and settings.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s4">
  <p class="st-k rv">04 · Key UX decisions</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s4">Three decisions that shaped the first run</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Three decisions shaped the first-run experience, each one shown on the screens it changed.
  </p>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision A</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">OTP via WhatsApp, with an email option</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">WhatsApp was the more feasible solution for the stakeholders.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/bloodconnect-flow.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1200"
        height="900"
        alt="Three registration screens: Create New Account, then entering a 4-digit authentication code sent by WhatsApp, and the same code sent by email"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Registration, with the code sent by WhatsApp or email (recreated with demo content)</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision B</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">NID upload at registration</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">It is there to help stop people from using the platform for scams.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision C</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Privacy and notification settings</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          They give people the freedom to set up the platform the way they want.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/bloodconnect-decisions.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1200"
        height="900"
        alt="Three screens: Add Personal Info with empty NID upload fields, the same form with blood group, donation date and both sides of the NID uploaded (blurred), and Settings with privacy and notification toggles"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>NID upload and settings on screen (recreated with demo content)</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s5">
  <p class="st-k rv">05 · Error states</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s5">Designing for mistakes, not only the happy path</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    We designed the error states alongside the happy path, including mandatory fields, a wrong OTP
    code and a password mismatch.
  </p>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Error 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Mandatory fields</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Log in flags both required fields when they are left empty.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Error 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Wrong OTP code</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">A wrong authentication code is caught on the code screen.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Error 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Password mismatch</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Set new password shows when the two passwords do not match.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure class="cs__fig st-wide rv">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/bloodconnect-errors.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1200"
      height="900"
      alt="Three error states: Log In with both mandatory fields flagged, a wrong authentication code, and Set New Password with a password mismatch"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>Error states designed alongside the happy path (recreated with demo content)</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

